 is also correct.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.3 – Claim that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered\n\nThe author analyses an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and splits into two cases.\n\n##### Case 1: \\(a\\le n-k\\)\n\n> “Then the vertical line \\(L_a\\) (which exists because \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\)) contains the point \\((a,b)\\).”\n\n**Verification:** Correct. If the \\(x\\)-coordinate is among the vertical lines, the point lies on that line.\n\n##### Case 2: \\(a> n-k\\)\n\nThe author writes \\(a=n-k+j\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and examines the sunny line \\(S_j\\).\n\n*First sub‑claim:*  \n\n> “By its definition,\n> \\[\n> b-1 = m_j\\bigl(a-(n-k+j)\\bigr)=m_j\\cdot 0 =0,\n> \\]\n> so \\(b=1\\).  \n> If \\(b=1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) is precisely the point \\(P_j\\) through which \\(S_j\\) was drawn, hence belongs to \\(S_j\\).”\n\n**Verification:** The algebraic step is correct **only when** the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(S_j\\). The equation \\(b-1 = m_j(a-(n-k+j))\\) is the condition for a point to be on \\(S_j\\). Substituting \\(a=n-k+j\\) indeed forces the right‑hand side to be zero, yielding \\(b=1\\). Thus the line \\(S_j\\) certainly contains the point \\((a,1)\\). However, this does **not** show that *any* point with the same \\(a\\) but \\(b\\neq 1\\) lies on \\(S_j\\).\n\n*Second sub‑claim (the crucial error):*  \n\n> “If \\(b>1\\) we note that, because \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\),\n> \\[\n> b\\le n+1-a = n+1-(n-k+j)=k+1-j .\n> \\]  \n> Choose the slopes \\(m_j\\) large enough (e.g. \\(m_j\\ge k\\)) so that the line \\(S_j\\) passes through **all** points \\((a,b)\\) with the same abscissa \\(a=n-k+j\\) and ordinate \\(1\\le b\\le k+1-j\\).  \n> Indeed, the line \\(S_j\\) contains the two points  \n> \\[\n> P_j=(a,1),\\qquad Q_j=(a,\\;k+1-j),\n> \\]\n> so its equation is exactly the one given above, and every intermediate integer ordinate \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le k+1-j\\) satisfies the line equation.  \n> Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_j\\).”\n\n**Verification – Critical Error:**  \nA line with finite, non‑zero slope can intersect a given vertical line \\(x=a\\) at **exactly one** point. The points \\(P_j=(a,1)\\) and \\(Q_j=(a,k+1-j)\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate but different \\(y\\)-coordinates (unless \\(k+1-j=1\\)). No non‑vertical line can pass through both of them simultaneously. The only line that contains both points is the **vertical line** \\(x=a\\), which has undefined slope and is therefore **non‑sunny**. Consequently the claim that the sunny line \\(S_j\\) contains both \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\) is false.  \n\nBecause a single sunny line cannot cover more than one point with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the construction fails to cover the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b>1\\) in Case 2. The attempted remedy of “choosing slopes large enough’’ does not alter this geometric impossibility; the slope magnitude does not affect the fact that a non‑vertical line meets a vertical line at a unique point.\n\nThus the central argument that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered is invalid for any \\(k\\ge 1\\). The only case where the construction works is \\(k=0\\) (all lines vertical), which indeed covers all points, but the solution claims validity for all \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### Step 3 – Conclusion drawn by the author\n\n> “For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines … Hence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).”\n\n**Verification:** Because the construction for \\(k\\ge 1\\) is flawed (as shown above), the conclusion is unsupported. The solution does not actually demonstrate the existence of the required families of lines for those \\(k\\). Therefore the final answer is not proven.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution correctly establishes the trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) and provides a valid construction for the extreme case 