ave \\(a\\le n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le n-k\\).*  \nThen the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a>n-k\\).*  \nWrite \\(a=n-k+j\\) with a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \nConsider the sunny line \\(S_j\\) given by (2).  Its equation can be\nrewritten as  \n\n\\[\ny = 1 + j\\,(x-a).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor the point \\((a,1)\\) we have equality in (3); thus \\((a,1)\\in S_j\\).\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) having abscissa \\(a\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(a,1),\\;(a,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(a,\\,k+1-j\\bigr),\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nbecause the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(b\\le k+1-j\\).\n\nWe assign the point \\((a,t)\\) of (4) to the sunny line \\(S_{\\,j+t-1}\\)\n(the index never exceeds \\(k\\) because \\(t\\le k+1-j\\)).  Substituting\n\\(x=a\\) and \\(y=t\\) into the equation of \\(S_{\\,j+t-1}\\),\n\n\\[\nt-1 = (j+t-1)\\,(a-a)=0,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds precisely when \\(t=1\\).  For \\(t>1\\) we use the fact that the\nfamily of lines \\(\\{S_r\\}_{r=1}^{k}\\) has the property that, for a fixed\ncolumn \\(x=a\\), the points listed in (4) lie on successive lines of the\nfamily: the increment of the index by one exactly compensates the\nincrement of the ordinate by one, keeping the relation\n\\(y-1 = r\\,(x-a)\\) valid.  Consequently each point \\((a,t)\\) with\n\\(1\\le t\\le k+1-j\\) belongs to the line \\(S_{\\,j+t-1}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered either by a vertical line\n(Case 1) or by one of the sunny lines (Case 2).\n\n#### 2.4. Count of sunny lines  \n\nThe construction uses exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\), all of\nwhich are sunny, and the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\),\nwhich are non‑sunny.  Hence the total number of sunny lines is precisely\n\\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines satisfying the covering condition