em parallel to the three forbidden directions; a convenient choice is the family of lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = m_i x + c_i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k),\n   \\]\n\n   where the slopes \\(m_i\\) are distinct rational numbers different from \\(0,-1\\) and the intercepts \\(c_i\\) are chosen so that each line passes through a distinct lattice point among the required set.  \n   Because the slopes are distinct, each sunny line can be made to pass through at most one of the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); the remaining points are then covered by the non\u2011sunny lines.  \n\n   The non\u2011sunny lines are taken from the three forbidden directions exactly as in the construction for \\(k=0\\); we use enough of them to cover all points not already hit by the sunny lines.  \n   A counting argument shows that, for any prescribed \\(k\\ge 2\\), the number of uncovered points after placing the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most \\(n-k\\), so we can always finish the covering with \\(n-k\\) additional non\u2011sunny lines.  \n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Suppose exactly one line were sunny.  Because a sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, it can contain at most one of the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) (otherwise two of those points would have the same difference \\(b-a\\) and would force the line to be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Hence the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines would have to cover at least \\(\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}-1\\) points, but each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) of them (a vertical line covers at most \\(n\\) points, a horizontal line at most \\(n\\), and a diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) at most \\(n\\)).  For \\(n\\ge 3\\) this is impossible, giving a contradiction.  \n\nTogether these arguments establish the exact set of admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a\\bigr)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), or has equation \\(x+y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine the following set of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\n\\Bigl\\{\\,x=t\\mid 1\\le t\\le n\\,\\Bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\Bigl\\{\\,y=t\\mid 1\\le t\\le n\\,\\Bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\Bigl\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies at least one of the three equations:\n\n* if \\(a=n+1-b\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\);\n* otherwise \\(a\\neq n+1-b\\).  If \\(a\\le n\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\);  \n  if \\(b\\le n\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nSince \\(|\\mathcal L_0|=2n+1>n\\), we may delete any \\(n+1\\) lines while preserving the covering property: delete the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=2,\\dots ,n\\) and the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=2,\\dots ,n\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n\\) lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}\\cup\\{x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\cup\\{y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich are distinct, non\u2011sunny, and still cover every point of \\(S_n\\).  \nHence a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose \\(k\\) distinct rational numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_1,m_2,\\dots ,m_k\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\n\\]\n\nand \\(k\\) distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in S_n\\) (possible because \\(|S_n|\\ge k\\)).  \nFor each \\(i\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=m_i x + (b_i-m_i a_i).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(L_i\\) are pairwise non\u2011parallel and none of them is parallel to the three forbidden directions; consequently each \\(L_i\\) is sunny and passes through the prescribed point \\((a_i,b_i)\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT=S_n\\setminus\\{(a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_k,b_k)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points still uncovered after placing the sunny lines.  \nWe have  \n\n\\[\n|T|=|S_n|-k=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWe now cover \\(T\\) using only non\u2011sunny lines.  \nObserve that a vertical line \\(x=t\\) (with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\)) contains exactly the points \\(\\{(t,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-t\\}\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\); the same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=t\\).  \nThe diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains exactly the points \\(\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\), also at most \\(n\\) points.\n\nHence each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T\\).  \nSince we are allowed to use exactly \\(n-k\\) additional lines, the total number of points that can be covered by them is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\cdot n = n^2 - kn .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) we need to cover  \n\n\\[\n|T|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k\n\\]\n\npoints.  For \\(k\\ge 2\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k\\le n^2-kn,\n\\]\n\nbecause the inequality is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^2-(k-1)n\n\\Longleftrightarrow\n\\frac{n+1}{2}\\le n-(k-1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nk\\le \\frac{n+1}{2}+1\\le n,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds for all admissible \\(k\\).  Consequently the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can be chosen so as to cover all points of \\(T\\).\n\nA concrete way to select them is the following greedy algorithm:\n\n* For each remaining integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) add the vertical line \\(x=t\\) if it contains at least one uncovered point of \\(T\\);\n* after processing all vertical lines, add horizontal lines \\(y=t\\) for those \\(t\\) that still contain uncovered points;\n* finally, if any points of \\(T\\) remain, add the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nBecause each step removes at least one uncovered point and we never use more than \\(n-k\\) lines (the total number of distinct vertical, horizontal and diagonal lines is exactly \\(2n+1\\), and we have already used \\(k\\) sunny lines), the algorithm succeeds.  \n\nThus we have constructed a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (Impossibility of \\(k=1\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly one sunny line, say \\(L\\).  \n\n*Because \\(L\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\).*\n\nIf \\(L\\) contained two distinct points \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in S_n\\), then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-b'}{a-a'}=m .\n\\]\n\nSince both points satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a'+b'\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n(b-b')=-(a-a')\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad m=-1,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting the choice of \\(L\\).  Hence \\(L\\) can contain **at most one** point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nConsequently the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover at least  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-1=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-1\n\\]\n\npoints.  Each non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (as observed in Lemma\u202f2).  Therefore the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\,n=n^2-n .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-1 > n^2-n,\n\\]\n\nbecause the inequality simplifies to \\(n^2-n-2>0\\), which holds for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Hence the required covering is impossible when exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nLemmas\u202f1,\u202f2 and\u202f3 together show that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad 2\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus the answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}\\cup\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
