0\\) is possible.\n\n3. **\\(k=1\\) is impossible.**  \n   A sunny line cannot be parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, therefore it can contain **at most one** point of \\(S_n\\) (otherwise two points of \\(S_n\\) would have the same difference \\(b-a\\) and the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Consequently the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover at least  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|-1=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-1\n   \\]\n\n   points.  \n   Each non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (a vertical, a horizontal or the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) meets at most \\(n\\) points of the set).  \n   Hence the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\((n-1)n=n^{2}-n\\) points.  \n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-1>n^{2}-n,\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  Thus no configuration with exactly one sunny line exists.\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n   *Step 1 – choose the sunny lines.*  \n   Pick any \\(k-1\\) distinct integers \\(t_{1},\\dots ,t_{k-1}\\) with \\(1\\le t_{i}\\le n-1\\).  \n   For each \\(t_{i}\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\; y = \\alpha_{i}x + \\beta_{i},\n   \\qquad\n   \\alpha_{i}\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0,-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   where the coefficients are chosen so that \\(L_{i}\\) passes through the single point \\((t_{i},\\,n+1-t_{i})\\in S_n\\) and is not parallel to any other \\(L_{j}\\).  \n   (Because the slopes \\(\\alpha_{i}\\) are distinct rational numbers, the lines are pairwise non‑parallel and each is sunny.)\n\n   *Step 2 – keep a “master” vertical line.*  \n   Keep the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V:\\; x=n .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   This line is non‑sunny and contains the \\(n\\) points \\((n,1),\\dots ,(n,n)\\) of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – fill the remaining columns with vertical lines.*  \n   For every integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) and \\(c\\neq t_{1},\\dots ,t_{k-1}\\) add the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   X_{c}