\\[\na-b \\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nChoose \\(i=a-b+1\\) (which lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)).  Substituting \\(i\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb = a-i+1 \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y = x-i+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).  Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  General construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe use two families of lines:\n\n* **Sunny lines** (slope \\(1\\)):\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y = x-i+1 ,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n  \\]\n  These are exactly the first \\(k\\) lines from the construction of §3; each is sunny.\n\n* **Non‑sunny vertical lines**:\n  \\[\n  V_j:\\; x=j ,\\qquad j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n .\n  \\]\n  All these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.\n\nWe have \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Coverage proof\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a-b\\le k-1\\).*  \nThen \\(i:=a-b+1\\) satisfies \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  By the definition of \\(L_i\\),\n\n\\[\nb = a-i+1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (a,b)\\in L_i .\n\\]\n\nThus the point lies on one of the sunny lines.\n\n*If \\(a-b\\ge k\\).*  \nSince \\(b\\ge 1\\), we have \\(a\\ge k+1\\).  Moreover, from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n\\).  \nConsequently the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which exists because \\(a\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)) contains the point:\n\\[\n(a,b)\\in V_a .\n\\]\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and  \n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTogether with the obvious bound \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), this shows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
