"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have a complete, rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The covering condition requires that every point of \\(T_n\\) lie on at least one of the chosen \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   \\(|T_n|=\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) (the triangular lattice of side\u2011length \\(n\\)).\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Non\u2011sunny* lines are exactly the vertical lines \\(x=c\\), the horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) and the lines of slope \\(-1\\) (the diagonals \\(x+y=c\\)).  \n   *Sunny* lines have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   A sunny line meets each of the \\(n-1\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_j=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a+b=j\\},\\qquad j=2,3,\\dots ,n\n   \\]\n\n   in **at most one** lattice point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *Proof.*  Since the slope is not \\(-1\\), the line cannot be parallel to any \\(D_j\\); two distinct points of the same diagonal have the same sum \\(a+b\\), hence lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\).  Therefore a line of a different slope can intersect a given diagonal in at most one point. \u220e\n\n   Consequently a sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (one from each diagonal).\n\n   *Lemma 2.*  \n   Any non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *Proof.*  A vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets the triangle in the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such points.  The same bound holds for horizontal lines and for diagonals \\(x+y=c\\). \u220e\n\n   Suppose that \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines are used. Then at most one line is non\u2011sunny.  \n   The total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{n}_{\\