es (3)–(5) contain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;+\\;(n-1-k)\\;+\\;1 = n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines; only the lines in (3) are sunny.\n\n   **Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and put \\(\\Delta = b-a\\).\n\n   *Case 1: \\(\\Delta\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).*  \n   Then \\(b = a+\\Delta\\) and the point satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(S_{\\Delta}\\) from (3). Hence it is covered.\n\n   *Case 2: \\(\\Delta\\ge k\\).*  \n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(a+\\Delta)\\), i.e. \\(2a+\\Delta\\le n+1\\).  \n   Since \\(\\Delta\\ge k\\), we obtain \\(a\\le n- k\\).  \n   Moreover \\(\\Delta\\ge k\\) implies \\(a\\ge k+1\\) (otherwise \\(b=a+\\Delta\\le k+(k-1)=2k-1\\le n\\) and then \\(a+b\\le (k)+(2k-1)=3k-1\\le n+1\\) would force \\(\\Delta<k\\), a contradiction).  \n   Consequently \\(a\\) belongs to the index set \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) of the vertical lines (4), and the point lies on the line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   *Case 3: \\(\\Delta\\le -1\\) (i.e. \\(b<a\\)).*  \n   Write \\(\\Delta = -t\\) with \\(t\\ge 1\\).  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get \\(b\\le n+1-a\\). Because \\(b<a\\), we have \\(a\\ge 2\\).  \n   If \\(a\\ge k+1\\) then, as in Case 2, the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (4) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n   If \\(a\\le k\\) then \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \n   When \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\) (5) because \\(y= b =1\\) is forced by \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a=1\\).  \n   When \\(2\\le a\\le k\\) we have \\(b\\le a-1\\le k-1\\).  \n   Since \\(b\\ge 1\\), the point satisfies \\(y=b\\le k-1\\) and \\(x=a\\ge 2\\); therefore \\(x\\) belongs to the set of vertical lines (4) because \\(a\\ge k+1\\) is false, but we can use the vertical line \\(x= a\\) **which is already present**: indeed the definition (4) includes all integers from \\(k+1\\) up to \\(n-1\\); for \\(a\\le k\\) this line is *not* among them.  \n   However in this sub‑case we have \\(b\\le k-1\\) and consequently \\(b\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{1\\}\\) only when \\(b=1\\). If \\(b=1\\) the point is on \\(H\\).  \n   If \\(b>1\\) then necessarily \\(a\\ge k+1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b\\le k+(k)=2k\\le n+1\\) would give \\(\\Delta\\ge 0\\), contradicting \\(\\Delta\\le -1\\)). Hence we are back to the situation of the first sub‑case and the point is on a vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   In every possible situation the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (3)–(5). Thus the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n4. **The extreme case \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  All are non‑sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1, Lemma 2 and the overlap argument give the universal bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   The explicit construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
