nes are non‑sunny, hence belong to \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\).\n\n   For a diagonal \\(D_s\\) of size \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) we have two possibilities:\n\n   * either the diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\) is present among the non‑sunny lines (then **all** points of \\(D_s\\) are already covered);\n   * or the diagonal line is absent. In that case every point of \\(D_s\\) must be covered by a sunny line, and because a sunny line contributes at most one point of \\(D_s\\), we need at least \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n   Hence, if the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) is omitted we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge s-1 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Consequences of (1).**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1\\text{ and the line }x+y=s\\text{ is omitted}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By (1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge \\max_{s\\in S}(s-1). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Conversely, if a diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) satisfies \\(s-1\\le k\\) we are allowed to omit it, because (1) is then satisfied.\n\n   Therefore the *only* diagonals that must be kept are those with size larger than \\(k\\); i.e. we must keep all lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=s\\qquad\\text{for }s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The number of such mandatory diagonals is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n+1)-(k+2)+1 = n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines can exist **iff** we can choose the remaining \\(k\\) lines (all sunny) so that they cover the points belonging to the omitted diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{k+1}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).**  \n\n   *Diagonal part.*  \n   Keep the \\(n-k\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   These are non‑sunny and together cover all points of the diagonals \\(D_{k+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,x+\\frac{i}{k+1-i}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,1)\\in D_{i+1}\\) and also through the point \\((1,i)\\in D_{i+1}\\); its slope equals \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{1-i}=-1\\) **only when** \\(i=1\\). To avoid the forbidden slope \\(-1\\) we modify the definition for \\(i=1\\) by taking  \n\n   \\[\n   S_1:\\; y=\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac12 ,\n   \\]\n\n   which passes through \\((1,1)\\in D_{2}\\) and is clearly sunny.  \n   For \\(i\\ge 2\\) the slope in (4) is \\(\\dfrac{i}{k+1-i}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   - For every \\(s\\in\\{k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the diagonal line \\(L_s\\) contains the whole set \\(D_s\\).  \n   - For each omitted diagonal \\(D_{i+1}\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) the point \\((i,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\); consequently every point of \\(D_{i+1}\\) is covered because \\(|D_{i+1}|=i\\) and we have exactly \\(i\\) distinct sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_i\\), each providing a different point of \\(D_{i+1}\\). (Explicitly, the points \\((1, i), (2, i-1),\\dots ,(i,1)\\) are taken by \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_i\\) respectively.)  \n\n   Hence the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{L_s\\mid s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\cup\\{S_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\((n-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). (For \\(k=0\\) the sunny part is empty; for \\(k=n\\) the diagonal part is empty and we keep only the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\).)\n\n6. **Upper bound.**  \n   From (2) we have \\(k\\ge \\max_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\). Since the largest possible value of \\(\\max_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\) is \\(n\\) (when all diagonals are omitted), the inequality imposes no restriction beyond \\(k\\le n\\) (the total number of lines). Therefore the necessary condition derived in §3 coincides exactly with the sufficient cons