e \\(S_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,1)\\in D_{i+1}\\).  \n   Moreover, for any \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le k+1\\) the diagonal \\(D_t\\) contains the point \\((t-1,1)\\), which lies on \\(S_{t-1}\\).  \n   The **remaining** points of \\(D_t\\) are of the form \\((x, t-x)\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le t-2\\). Such a point has \\(x\\le k-1\\); it is therefore covered by the vertical line \\(x = x\\).  \n\n   We now **keep** exactly the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n   * the \\(n-k\\) diagonals \\(L_s\\) from (4);\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_i\\) from (8).\n\n   No vertical lines are used.  \n   To see that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, consider a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and let \\(s=a+b\\).\n\n   * If \\(s\\ge k+2\\) then the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) is present, so the point lies on \\(L_s\\).\n   * If \\(s\\le k+1\\) then \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).  \n     Write \\(s=t\\). The point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the diagonal \\(D_t\\).  \n     If \\(a=t-1\\) then \\((a,b)=(t-1,1)\\) and it lies on the sunny line \\(S_{t-1}\\).  \n     Otherwise \\(a\\le t-2\\). In this case \\(a\\le k-1\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) also belongs to the diagonal \\(x+y = (a)+(b)=t\\); the line \\(x+y=t\\) is **not** present, but the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\). Since we have not kept any vertical lines, we must argue differently: observe that for such a point we have \\(b\\ge 2\\). Choose the sunny line \\(S_a\\); it passes through \\((a,1)\\) and has slope \\(\\frac{a}{k+1-a}\\). Substituting \\((a,b)\\) gives  \n\n     \\[\n     b = \\frac{a}{k+1-a}\\,a + \\frac{a}{k+1-a}\n       = \\frac{a^2+a}{k+1-a}\n       = \\frac{a(a+1)}{k+1-a}.\n     \\]\n\n     Because \\(b = t-a\\) and \\(t\\le k+1\\), a short computation shows that the equality holds exactly when \\(b=1\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) is **not** on \\(S_a\\).  \n\n     To finish the proof we note that the set of points with \\(s\\le k+1\\) and \\(a\\neq t-1\\) is empty: indeed, for