  \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Moreover, (2) shows that **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines are necessary when \\(n\\ge 4\\); otherwise the left\u2013hand side would be at most \\(n-1\\) while the right\u2013hand side is \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>n-1\\). Consequently the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-2\\).\n\n3. **Existence of configurations with \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The construction in Section\u202f1 of the detailed solution provides, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), a family of exactly \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines (vertical, horizontal and diagonal) that covers all points of \\(T_n\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n4. **Existence of configurations with \\(k=n-2\\) (for \\(n\\ge 4\\)).**  \n   Take the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) either lies on one of these two lines (those with a coordinate equal to\u202f1) or belongs to a set  \n   \\[\n   P_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad 4\\le s\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n   For each \\(s\\) the set \\(P_s\\) contains at most two points; we choose a **sunny** line \\(S_s\\) passing through exactly the points of \\(P_s\\) (possible because any two distinct points determine a line whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   The lines \\(\\{S_s\\mid 4\\le s\\le n+1\\}\\) are distinct, there are \\(n-2\\) of them, and together with \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) they give a family of \\(n\\) lines with precisely \\(n-2\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n5. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   Here \\(|T_3|=3\\). Because a sunny line can contain at most two points, three lines are sufficient to cover \\(T_3\\) even if all of them are sunny (simply let each line pass through a single point of \\(T_3\\)).  \n   Conversely, the constructions of Sections\u202f3 and\u202f4 give configurations with \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) (take the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\) and a third sunny line through the remaining point).  \n   By adding or removing sunny lines while keeping the total number of lines equal to three, one can realise every intermediate value of \\(k\\). Hence for \\(n=3\\) all integers \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are possible.\n\nCombining the existence results with the upper bound (3) yields the complete classification stated in the theorem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the integer points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nThus the non\u2011sunny lines are exactly the vertical lines \\(x=c\\), the horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) and the diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) (with \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n\n*Each sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).*  \nIndeed, if a sunny line contained three points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\), the three points would be collinear with slope either \\(0\\) (if two have the same \\(y\\)), \\(\\infty\\) (if two have the same \\(x\\)) or \\(-1\\) (if the sum of the coordinates is constant), contradicting the definition of sunny.\n\nHence the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe remaining \\(|T_n|-2k\\) points must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\):  \nthe vertical line \\(x=1\\) (or the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)) meets \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), i.e. \\(n-1\\) points, and no non\u2011sunny line can meet more points because any point of \\(T_n\\) has coordinates at most \\(n\\).\n\nTherefore we must have  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)(n-1)\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|-2k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (1) into (2) and simplifying:\n\n\\[\n(n-k)(n-1)\\ge \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}-2k\n\\Longrightarrow\n2(n-k)(n-1)\\ge n(n-1)-4k\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\; 2n(n-1)-2k(n-1)\\ge n(n-1)-4k\n\\Longrightarrow\\; n(n-1)-2k(n-1)+4k\\ge 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\; n(n-1)-2k(n-3)\\ge 0 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\), the coefficient \\(n-3\\) is non\u2011negative, and we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2k\\le n-2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.  \n\nMoreover, when \\(n\\ge 4\\) the inequality (2) forces \\(n-k\\ge 2\\); i.e. at least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary. Consequently the maximal attainable number of sunny lines is exactly \\(n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction with \\(k=0\\) (all lines non\u2011sunny)\n\nDefine three families of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal V &=\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\,\\},\\\\[2mm]\n\\mathcal H &=\\{\\,y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\,\\},\\\\[2mm]\n\\mathcal D &=\\{\\,x+y=n+1-\\ell\\mid \\ell=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\,\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) have slopes \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence they are non\u2011sunny.  \nCounting:\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal V|=|\\mathcal H|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\qquad\n|\\mathcal D|=n-2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n\\]\nso the total number of lines is \\(n\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\).\n* If \\(b\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\).\n* If both \\(a,b>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) then \\(a+b\\ge 2\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+2\\).  \n  Because \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence\n  \\[\n  n+1-(a+b)\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\},\n  \\]\n  and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1-(a+b)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal D\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and the configuration contains **no sunny line**. Hence \\(k=0\\) is feasible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction with \\(k=n-2\\) (exactly two non\u2011sunny lines) for \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nTake the two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first or second coordinate equals\u202f1.\n\nFor the remaining points, note that for each integer \\(s\\) with \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nP_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains at most two points:\n\n* if \\(s\\) is even, \\(P_s=\\{(\\tfrac{s}{2},\\tfrac{s}{2})\\}\\);\n* if \\(s\\) is odd, \\(P_s=\\{(\\tfrac{s-1}{2},\\tfrac{s+1}{2}),(\\tfrac{s+1}{2},\\tfrac{s-1}{2})\\}\\).\n\nChoose, for each such \\(s\\), a sunny line \\(S_s\\) passing through the points of \\(P_s\\) (any two distinct points determine a line whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)).  \nBecause the sets \\(P_s\\) are disjoint, the lines \\(\\{S_s\\mid 4\\le s\\le n+1\\}\\) are distinct.  \n\nThe number of values of \\(s\\) is  \n\n\\[\n(n+1)-3 = n-2 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,S_s\\mid 4\\le s\\le n+1\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (points with a coordinate\u202f1 are covered by \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\); all others belong to some \\(P_s\\) and are covered by the corresponding sunny line \\(S_s\\)), and has precisely \\(n-2\\) sunny lines. Hence \\(k=n-2\\) is attainable for all \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The exceptional case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\},\\qquad |T_3|=3 .\n\\]\n\n*All\u2011sunny configuration.*  \nChoose three distinct sunny lines, each passing through exactly one of the three points (any line through a single point can be given a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)). This yields a configuration with \\(k=3\\).\n\n*Zero sunny lines.*  \nUsing the construction of Section\u202f2 with \\(n=3\\) we obtain the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=3\\); thus \\(k=0\\) is possible.\n\n*One sunny line.*  \nTake the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\) and a third sunny line through the remaining point \\((2,1)\\); this gives \\(k=1\\).\n\n*Two sunny lines.*  \nReplace the diagonal line \\(x+y=3\\) of the zero\u2011sunny configuration by a sunny line through the two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); now exactly two of the three lines are sunny, i.e. \\(k=2\\).\n\nHence for \\(n=3\\) every integer \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) can be realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nCombining the upper bound (3) with the explicit constructions:\n\n* For \\(n\\ge 4\\) the only admissible values are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n-2\\).\n* For the special case \\(n=3\\) all values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) occur.\n\nThus the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the theorem. \u220e"
