            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nWe must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, each of which is either sunny or non‑sunny.  \nHence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A covering with only non‑sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nConsider the following family of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k\\,\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are either vertical or horizontal, therefore non‑sunny.\n\n*Claim.* Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nIf \\(a\\le k\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_k\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \nIf \\(a>k\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(k+1)=n-k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n-k\\), and the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_k\\) contains \\((a,b)\\). ∎\n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) is a valid configuration with **zero** sunny lines when \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Replacing vertical lines by sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\ge 1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover \\(\\sigma_i\\) passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,0)\\).\n\nObserve that the point \\((i,1)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=i\\) of the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \nIf we replace the line \\(x=i\\) by \\(\\sigma_i\\), the point \\((i,1)\\) remains covered (now by \\(\\sigma_i\\)).  \nAny other point \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) is still covered by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which is **not** altered by the replacement.  \nTherefore the covering property of the family is preserved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nLet the desired number of sunny lines be an arbitrary integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Construction.*  \nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) (which consists of the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\)).  \nChoose any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).  \nReplace each vertical line \\(x=i\\) for \\(i\\in I\\) by the sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\) defined in (4).  \nAll vertical lines \\(x=j\\) with \\(j\\notin I\\) are kept.  \n\nThe resulting family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(\\sigma_i\\) with \\(i\\in I\\)) and \\(n-k\\) vertical lines, hence \\(n\\) distinct lines in total.\n\n*Verification of the covering.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n\\) and the vertical line \\(x=a\\) was **not** replaced, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on that vertical line.  \n* If \\(x=a\\) was replaced (i.e. \\(a\\in I\\)), then \\((a,1)\\) lies on \\(\\sigma_a\\).  \n  For \\(b=1\\) we are done.  \n  For \\(b\\ge 2\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is **not** present in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\); however, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge 1\\), we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\).  \n  Since the family still contains the vertical line \\(x=n+1-b\\) (which was never replaced because \\(n+1-b\\notin I\\) when \\(b\\ge 2\\)), the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on that vertical line: indeed \\(a\\le n+1-b\\) follows from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* The trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\).  \n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
