tisfying \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\Longrightarrow -(n-1)\\le b-a\\le n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The \\(n\\) values \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) cover all possible positive differences \\(b-a\\) that can occur for points in the triangular region; the remaining points with \\(b-a\\le 0\\) are covered by the lines \\(S_{-t}\\) with \\(t\\ge 1\\), but we do not need them because every point with \\(b-a\\le 0\\) already lies on one of the lines \\(S_t\\) for a suitable \\(t\\) (take \\(t=1\\) and shift the point horizontally). A more direct argument is:\n\n   *For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the diagonal \\(a+b=s\\) contains at least one point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b-a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); that point lies on the sunny line \\(S_{b-a}\\).*  \n\n   Consequently every required lattice point belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\). The lines are distinct and none of them is parallel to the forbidden directions, so \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n3. **Interpolation for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))** –  \n\n   *Construction.*  \n   - Choose \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\):\n\n     \\[\n     S_t:\\; y = x + t ,\\qquad t=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n     \\]\n\n   - Choose the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny vertical lines:\n\n     \\[\n     V_i:\\; x=i ,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k .\n     \\]\n\n   *Verification that every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is covered.*\n\n   - If \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   - If \\(a> n-k\\) then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(1\\le b\\le k\\). For such a point we have  \n\n     \\[\n     b-a = (b) - a = -(a-b) = -(a-b) .\n     \\]\n\n     Because \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(b\\le k\\),\n\n     \\[\n     a-b \\ge (n-k+1)-k = n-2k+1 .\n     \\]\n\n     In particular \\(b-a\\) is an integer between \\(-k\\) and \\(-1\\). Adding \\(k\\) to both sides gives  \n\n     \\[\n     (b-a)+k \\in \\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     Therefore the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies  \n\n     \\[\n     b = a + (b-a) = a + \\bigl[(b-a)+k\\bigr] - k = a + t - k ,\n     \\]\n\n     where \\(t:= (b-a)+k\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\). Rearranging,\n\n     \\[\n     b = a + t - k \\Longleftrightarrow b = a + (t-k) .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_t\\) (since \\(S_t\\) is defined by \\(y = x + t\\)).  \n\n     Consequently every point with \\(a>n-k\\) is covered by one of the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   The two families of lines are disjoint, all lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and together they contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nSince the construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), these are precisely the admissible values.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
