            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,n-k)                     \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\;y=x+\\bigl(n-2k+2i-1\\bigr)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(H_{j}\\) are horizontal, hence non‑sunny; all \\(L_{i}\\) have slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), hence are sunny.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines in (4)–(5) are distinct because the horizontal lines have different\n\\(y\\)‑coordinates and the lines \\(L_{i}\\) have different intercepts.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points with small \\(y\\)–coordinate\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) and \\(b\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line\n\\(H_{b}\\). Thus every point of  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in S_{n}\\mid b\\le n-k\\}\n\\]\n\nis covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Points with large \\(y\\)–coordinate  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{k}:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_{n}\\mid b\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{k}\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le k\\); together with\n\\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) this yields  \n\n\\[\nn-2k+1\\le b-a\\le n-1 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(c:=b-a\\). The integers satisfying (6) form the consecutive block  \n\n\\[\nn-2k+1,\\;n-2k+2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n\\]\n\nEvery integer \\(c\\) in this block can be expressed uniquely as  \n\n\\[\nc = \\bigl(n-2k+2i-1\\bigr)+\\varepsilon ,\\qquad\n1\\le i\\le k,\\;\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the odd numbers of the block are exactly the numbers\n\\(n-2k+2i-1\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)); each even number is obtained from the preceding odd\nnumber by adding \\(1\\).\n\nSubstituting \\(c=b-a\\) into (7) gives  \n\n\\[\nb = a + \\bigl(n-2k+2i-1\\bigr)+\\varepsilon .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\varepsilon=0\\) the point satisfies the equation of \\(L_{i}\\) from (5).  \nIf \\(\\varepsilon=1\\) then  \n\n\\[\nb = a + \\bigl(n-2k+2(i+1)-1\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nso the point satisfies the equation of \\(L_{i+1}\\) (the case \\(i=k\\) cannot occur\nbecause the maximal value of \\(c\\) is \\(n-1\\), which is odd). Consequently every\n\\((a,b)\\in T_{k}\\) lies on one of the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the covering\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}_{k}= \\{H_{1},\\dots ,H_{n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines. By Sections 2 and 3 every point of\n\\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Number of sunny lines\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines\nare horizontal and therefore not sunny. Hence the configuration realises the\nprescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Edge cases\n\n*If \\(k=0\\)*, the construction reduces to the \\(n\\) horizontal lines\n\\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\); no sunny line appears and the covering is obvious.\n\n*If \\(k=n\\)*, there are no horizontal lines.  The sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y = x + (2i-1),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(1\\le b-a\\le n\\) and, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\\(a\\) and \\(b\\) have opposite parity; therefore \\(b-a\\) is odd, say \\(b-a=2i-1\\),\nand the point lies on \\(L_{i}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Necessity of the bound\n\nSince the configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of\nsunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Thus no integer \\(k>n\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines, and we have shown that larger values of \\(k\\) are impossible.\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
