to the two disjoint subsets\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   O&:=\\{\\,n-2k+1,\\;n-2k+3,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}\\quad\\text{(odd numbers)},\\\\\n   E&:=\\{\\,n-2k+2,\\;n-2k+4,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}\\quad\\text{(even numbers)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   The set \\(O\\) has exactly \\(k\\) elements, namely\n   \\[\n   O=\\bigl\\{\\,n-2k+2i-1\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   The set \\(E\\) also has exactly \\(k-1\\) elements; each even number can be\n   written uniquely as\n   \\[\n   c = 2a + \\bigl(n-3k+3i-2\\bigr)\n   \\quad\\text{with}\\quad\n   a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\},\\; i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\},\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   because solving \\(c=2a+d\\) for \\(d\\) gives \\(d=c-2a\\), and the possible\n   values of \\(d\\) are precisely the numbers \\(n-3k+3i-2\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)).\n   (A short verification shows that as \\(a\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\) and\n   \\(i\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\) the expression \\(2a+(n-3k+3i-2)\\) produces\n   exactly the even integers in (4).)\n\n   Now let \\((a,b)\\in T_{k}\\).  \n   *If \\(c=b-a\\) is odd*, then by (5) there is a unique \\(i\\) with\n   \\(c=n-2k+2i-1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of\n   \\(L^{(1)}_{i}\\).  \n\n   *If \\(c=b-a\\) is even*, write \\(c\\) as in (6). Then\n   \\[\n   b = a + 2a + \\bigl(n-3k+3i-2\\bigr)\n     = 2a + \\bigl(n-3k+3i-2\\bigr),\n   \\]\n   which is exactly the defining equation of the line \\(L^{(2)}_{i}\\).\n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L^{(2)}_{i}\\).\n\n   In both cases the point belongs to one of the sunny lines, proving the\n   lemma. \u220e\n\n4. **Coverage of the \u201clow\u201d points.**  \n   If \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) and \\(b\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal\n   line \\(H_{b}\\) from (1). Thus all points of\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,b)\\in S_{n}\\mid b\\le n-k\\}\n   \\]\n   are covered.\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n   The family\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}_{k}= \\{H_{1},\\dots ,H_{n-k}\\}\n  