lines.  \n   *If \\(1\\le k\\le n\\)* we replace \\(k\\) disjoint pairs of consecutive vertical lines by the sunny lines \\(M_t\\) (step 3). This uses \\(n-k\\) lines. To reach the required total of \\(n\\) lines we add \\(k\\) distinct horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots , y=k\\). The added horizontal lines are non‑sunny, so the total number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\). All points of \\(S_n\\) are still covered because each point either lies on one of the unchanged vertical lines, on one of the sunny lines \\(M_t\\), or on one of the added horizontal lines (the latter are superfluous but harmless).\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must find, for a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that  \n\n1. \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\ell_i\\supseteq S_n\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny*, i.e. are **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n   \\[\n   x\\text{-axis }(y=0),\\qquad y\\text{-axis }(x=0),\\qquad x+y=0 .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence it is not sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on \\(L_i\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(i,1),\\;(i,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(i,\\,n+1-i\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nbecause the condition \\(i+b\\le n+1\\) is equivalent to \\(b\\le n+1-i\\).  \nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  \nConsequently the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition and contains **no** sunny line. Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A basic sunny line that can replace two vertical lines\n\nFor any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nM_t:\\; y = x-t+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(M_t\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(M_t\\) is sunny (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nThe line \\(M_t\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\n(t,1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (t+1,2),\n\\]\n\nboth of which belong to \\(S_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\nt+1\\le n+1,\\qquad (t+1)+2\\le n+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;t\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the two vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;x=t,\\qquad L_{t+1}:\\;x=t+1\n\\]\n\ncan be **simultaneously removed** and replaced by the single sunny line \\(M_t\\) without losing any point of \\(S_n\\): the points \\((t,1)\\) and \\((t+1,2)\\) remain covered (by \\(M_t\\)), while every other point that was on \\(L_t\\) or \\(L_{t+1}\\) lies on a different vertical line \\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\neq t,t+1\\).\n\nThus we have a *local transformation*:\n\n\\[\n\\{L_t,\\,L_{t+1}\\}\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\{M_t\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich\n\n* reduces the total number of lines by one,\n* introduces exactly one sunny line,\n* preserves the covering of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Constructing configurations with an arbitrary number of sunny lines\n\nStart from the base family \\(\\mathcal{F}_0=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) (all non‑sunny).  \nChoose any integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and apply the transformation of §2 to \\(r\\) **pairwise disjoint** pairs of consecutive vertical lines, e.g. to the pairs \\((L_1,L_2), (L_3,L_4),\\dots ,(L_{2r-1},L_{2r})\\).  \n\nAfter these \\(r\\) replacements we obtain a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}_r=\\{M_1,\\dots ,M_r\\}\\cup\\{L_{2r+1},L_{2r+2},\\dots ,L_n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of  \n\n* \\(r\\) sunny lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_r\\),\n* \\(n-2r\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines.\n\nThe total number of lines in \\(\\mathcal{F}_r\\) is \\(n-r\\).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) are still covered, because the argument of §2 guarantees that the points lost from the removed vertical lines are recovered on the sunny lines, and the remaining vertical lines continue to cover the rest of \\(S_n\\).\n\nIf we need **exactly** \\(n\\) distinct lines, we can simply add \\(r\\) further non‑sunny lines, for instance the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,r).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_j\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence it is not sunny, and it does not interfere with the covering property (it only adds extra coverage).  \n\nConsequently, for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we may set \\(r=k\\) and define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{G}_k=\\{M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\}\\cup\\{L_{2k+1},\\dots ,L_n\\}\\cup\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal{G}_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(M_i\\)’s), the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, and it has precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines. By the discussion above, \\(\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal{G}_k}\\ell\\supseteq S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n* a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines, and\n* for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore **every** integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is admissible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For a given }n\\ge 3\\text{, the possible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"
