 \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n   They cover \\(S_n\\) and are all non‑sunny.\n\n   *Introducing a sunny line.*  \n   For any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\) define the line  \n   \\[\n   S_t:\\; y = x-t+1 .\n   \\]\n   Its slope is \\(1\\); hence \\(S_t\\) is sunny.  \n   \\(S_t\\) passes through the two points \\((t,1)\\) and \\((t+1,2)\\), both belonging to \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Local replacement.*  \n   Replace the two vertical lines \\(V_t\\) and \\(V_{t+1}\\) by the single sunny line \\(S_t\\).  \n   The points \\((t,1)\\) and \\((t+1,2)\\) remain covered (by \\(S_t\\)); every other point of column \\(t\\) or \\(t+1\\) is still covered by the remaining vertical lines \\(V_i\\) with \\(i\\neq t,t+1\\).  \n   Thus the covering property is preserved, the total number of lines decreases by one, and exactly one sunny line is introduced.\n\n   *Iterating the replacement.*  \n   Choose any \\(k\\) distinct indices \\(t_1,\\dots ,t_k\\) such that the pairs \\(\\{t_j,t_j+1\\}\\) are disjoint (e.g. \\(t_j=2j-1\\)).  \n   Perform the above replacement on each of these \\(k\\) pairs.  \n   After the \\(k\\) replacements we have  \n\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_{t_1},\\dots ,S_{t_k}\\);\n   * \\(n-2k\\) untouched vertical lines;\n   * total number of lines \\(n-k\\).\n\n   *Restoring the total to \\(n\\).*  \n   Add \\(k\\) further non‑sunny lines, for instance the horizontal lines  \n   \\[\n   H_j:\\; y=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n   Horizontal lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and they do not destroy the covering (they only add extra coverage).  \n\n   The final family therefore consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and it still covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Suppose all \\(n\\) lines were sunny. Then none of them could be vertical. As argued in Lemma 2, the absence of a vertical line \\(x=i\\) forces at least \\(n+1-i\\) distinct sunny lines to cover column \\(i\\). Summing over \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) gives a lower bound of  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n}(n+1-i)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n   \\]\n\n   contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(n\\) lines are used. Hence at least one line must be non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\nCombining the construction (which works for every \\(k\\le n-1\\)) with the impossibility proof for \\(k=n\\) yields the desired classification.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,\\dots ,n-1.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
