\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell\\) is either vertical, horizontal or diagonal.\n\nNow replace \\(\\ell\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell' :\\; y-b_0 = m\\,(x-a_0),\n\\]\n\nwhere the integer slope \\(m\\) is chosen so that  \n\n* \\(\\ell'\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions (hence sunny),  \n* \\(\\ell'\\) does not coincide with any line already present in \\(\\mathcal C_t\\) (possible because there are infinitely many admissible slopes).\n\nThe new line \\(\\ell'\\) passes through the previously uncovered point \\(P_0\\); consequently \\(P_0\\) becomes covered by a sunny line.  \nAll other points that were covered by \\(\\ell\\) remain covered, because each of them also lies on at least one of the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal C_t\\) (the original configuration used exactly one line for each vertical coordinate, so removing a vertical line does not destroy the coverage of points with a different \\(x\\)-coordinate).  \n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{t+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal C_t\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(t+1\\) sunny lines and still covers all of \\(S_n\\).\n\nRepeating this replacement procedure \\(k\\) times yields a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for any prescribed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions for the extreme cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\), and we have shown a systematic method to increase the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering condition. Consequently, for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n\n* every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nTherefore the complete set o