ines that covers \\(T_n\\).  \nAssume that \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) is **not** sunny; then \\(\\ell\\) is of one of the following three types:\n\n* horizontal: \\(\\ell : y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\);\n* vertical:   \\(\\ell : x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\);\n* diagonal with slope \\(-1\\): \\(\\ell : x+y=t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\).\n\nIn each case \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) contains at least two points:\n\n* horizontal \\(y=c\\): the points \\((1,c)\\) and \\((2,c)\\) belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(1+c\\le n\\) and \\(2+c\\le n+1\\);\n* vertical \\(x=c\\): the points \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c,2)\\) belong to \\(T_n\\);\n* diagonal \\(x+y=t\\): the points \\((1,t-1)\\) and \\((2,t-2)\\) belong to \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nm:=|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\ge 2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsider the other \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nAny line that is not parallel to \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\ell\\) in at most one point; parallel lines are distinct from \\(\\ell\\) and therefore disjoint from it. Consequently each of the \\(n-1\\) other lines can contain **at most one** point of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\). Thus the total number of points of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) that are also on some other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is at most \\(n-1\\).\n\nIf every point of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) were covered by another line, we would have \\(m\\le n-1\\).  \nWhen \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal with \\(c\\le n-2\\) we have \\(m=n+1-c\\ge 3>n-1\\); similarly for vertical lines with \\(c\\le n-2\\) and for diagonal lines with \\(t\\le n-1\\) we obtain \\(m\\ge 3\\). Hence in all these cases \\(m>n-1\\) and therefore at least one point of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) is **not** on any other line.\n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) is one of the two “short” lines (the horizontal \\(y=n-1\\), the vertical \\(x=n-1\\) or the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\)), then \\(m=2\\). In this situation the other \\(n-1\\) lines cannot cover both points simultaneously, because two distinct points of \\(\\ell\\) would require two distinct other lines, and those two lines would then be parallel to \\(\\ell\\) (otherwise they would intersect \\(\\ell\\) in the same point). But the only lines parallel to \\(\\ell\\) that belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\) are the other horizontal (or vertical, or diagonal) lines, and there is at most one such parallel line in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (otherwise two parallel lines would coincide, contradicting distinctness). Hence at most one of the two points can be covered by another line, and the other point is uncovered.\n\nThus **in every case** there exists a point  \n\n\\[\nP\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\qquad\\text{that does not lie on any line of }\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nChoose a sunny slope \\(\\sigma\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (for definiteness take \\(\\sigma=1\\)).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell' :\\; y-P_y = \\sigma\\,(x-P_x),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(P=(P_x,P_y)\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell'\\) passes through \\(P\\) and has a sunny slope, so \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny and cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) either have a different slope or miss \\(P\\)).\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L' = (\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  By construction \\(\\ell'\\) is different from every line remaining in \\(\\mathcal L\\); thus \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \n- Every point of \\(T_n\\) that was covered by a line other than \\(\\ell\\) remains covered.  \n- The point \\(P\\) is now covered by \\(\\ell'\\).  \n- Any other point \\(Q\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) (different from \\(P\\)) was already covered by some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\): indeed, if \\(Q\\) were not, then both \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) would be uncovered, contradicting (2) which guarantees the existence of **exactly one** uncovered point.\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still covers all points of \\(T_n\\). Moreover the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L'\\) is one larger than in \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nStart with the configuration \\(\\mathcal B\\) of Lemma 1, which has \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nApply Lemma 2 repeatedly: each application replaces a non‑sunny line by a sunny one while preserving the covering property. After performing the replacement \\(k\\) times we obtain a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause the replacement can be carried out as long as a non‑sunny line remains, we can reach any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound\n\nThe problem stipulates that exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines must be used. Consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence no integer \\(k>n\\) is admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists are precisely those with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
